            <ArticleTitle>Imminent myocardial infarction: a psychological study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>129-34</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Unstable angina pectoris and feelings of fatigue and general malaise are often mentioned as premonitory symptoms of myocardial infarction. From a psychological point of view these feelings of fatigue and malaise reflect a syndrome of vital exhaustion and depression (VED). A questionnaire which measures this syndrome was given to 3,571 males who participated in a voluntary health check up. It was found that the prevalence of &quot;imminent myocardial infarction,&quot; defined as unstable angina pectoris plus electrocardiographic signs of ischaemia, was more than four times higher among exhausted and depressive persons, than among persons not so affected.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
